#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "81531c55-fe2e-5eff-82ba-9e08abf24a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6591" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-2y'+y = 0.$ If $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =  1$  is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "3363cf38-10a2-5a55-9451-ee2101b8e809" :question/math-academy-id "e-6592" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t} + 36y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t}= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2}= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+36{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =  6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 t} + Bte^{6t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "abc842b6-8b86-585c-a425-5776909c6425" :question/math-academy-id "e-6593" :question/problem "Given the equation $y''+12y'+36y = 0$  for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $4e^{-6x}-3xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $2e^{6x}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+36{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =-6$ is a repeated root.  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6 x} + Bxe^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=0$ and $B=1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=4$ and $B=-3.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^{6x},$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047d14b8-959a-5cc9-926b-38287d75c63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-10$ or $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263042} {:answer/id #uuid "11dd7fdd-ab31-5f4a-be88-bc9ac2bc37e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263166} {:answer/id #uuid "316b0aef-b294-5b23-9fe7-b39de3fb496e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263423} {:answer/id #uuid "8c52d2c0-8834-5565-832a-506d0c3d29cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264201} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa666a6-5cd7-5528-94f3-ec99f42f41a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa666a6-5cd7-5528-94f3-ec99f42f41a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265247} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2bb791-8c6e-5428-b65b-a7fb3dc1da50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f83753da-0895-5127-9ad2-0fef97323b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-130133" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-5$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa4ba32-b529-58e4-a4eb-63d647925307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-7x}+Bx{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354897} {:answer/id #uuid "4649425b-a01b-5129-9bd1-4382c61b2bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{14x}+Bx{e}^{14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355287} {:answer/id #uuid "9c22336b-7b6e-5da6-8b71-2c3603745b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-7x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356048} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6dfdd8-97b3-52a7-a2b7-629792936121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{7x}+B{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356201} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9e9c7e-d981-5152-80a6-2ec06872c639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{7x}+Bx{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea9e9c7e-d981-5152-80a6-2ec06872c639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{7x}+Bx{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356713} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e6b10f8-76e5-539d-b6d3-c339af0fce9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee8781b5-1cb6-5737-93a0-098c0c971d2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-90958" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-14\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+49y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+49{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)(\\lambda -7) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+Bx{e}^{7x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083e2e45-0943-5269-9ed1-be04b8166fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354736} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1a3469-60bb-5bdd-bef9-07d04acd0f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8x}-x{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355928} {:answer/id #uuid "afd94002-d20c-520b-82fa-24b044ec8b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{8x}+2x{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356211} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f52321-e6c1-5fe1-93d9-3280bac0f8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356632} {:answer/id #uuid "e91a4495-f899-526a-80f8-9cfea704eb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0f52321-e6c1-5fe1-93d9-3280bac0f8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356632} :answer-field/id #uuid "5079ed97-fbbb-51bc-89cc-fdb0086f4164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ced6ca5a-46ff-55d4-9045-c405e7b10434" :question/math-academy-id "q-90966" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-16{y}^{′}+64y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-16\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+64{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda -8)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =8$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{8x}$ and $x{e}^{8x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${x}^{2}{e}^{8x}$ is not a solution."}]}
